Z||000000||batboy||02-18-2000||09:07 AM||||The next nice weather we get, I'm ready to tackle the rear bushings and replacing the rear ball joints. I've seriously considered getting rear coil-overs and now I've seen this bump steer eliminator kit. Since I have a V8, bump steer is a real problem. Is this over-kill for the street? Is it worth while? Should I just put the poly bushings in the rear control arms that I already have and go with the coil-overs?<P> <A HREF="http://www.ryanemotorsports.com/Bump.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.ryanemotorsports.com/Bump.htm</A> <p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 02-18-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||Phil||02-18-2000||10:08 AM||||Bat<BR>RCC Specialties also has a bump steer kit for about $120. So for about $350 you could have coil-overs and bump steer correction. Just something to check on.||||reg||1
Z||000002||batboy||02-18-2000||10:28 AM||||Phil, I can't find the bump steer kit on the RCC webpage or am I looking in the wrong place?<P> <A HREF="http://www.v8archie.com/rcc/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.v8archie.com/rcc/</A> <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Monkeyman||02-18-2000||11:05 AM||||What exactly is bump steer?||||reg||1
Z||000004||batboy||02-18-2000||11:33 AM||||Monkey, read that Ryane link (above). It'll explain it better than I can. If I tromp down hard on the gas at cruising speed, the whole back end of my car shifts to the left momentarily. Gotta pay attention and even then, it's a handful with all that torque of a V8. Part of it is worn out bushings, but part of it is the nature of the transverse mid-engine Fiero design which is really a modified front wheel drive unit. A rear sway bar will help, but if I can find a cheap fix that improves this problem even more, than yippee!||||reg||1
Z||000006||Phil||02-18-2000||11:51 AM||||Bat <BR>E-mail or call them and ask. It's in their  "catalog" and I have talked to them on another matter and we spoke briefly about the kit $120. But they want $400 for their coil-over rear and Rayne gets $220(I think). I put Rayne's coil-over system on my kit car, the car is not finished so can't say how they work but installation was easy enough and very easy to adjust ride height. You only have to remove the spring perch on your original struts, slide on the threaded tube and bolt on the new top and TA DA you're done.||||reg||1
Z||000007||batboy||02-18-2000||12:18 PM||||Ok Phil, I found the bump steer kit listed in the RCC section of the Big Fiero Book for, like you said, $120. It's not a complete control arm replacement like the Ryane's kit, but much cheaper. This might be an old listing. I'll email them and see if they still carry them.<P>I'll probably go with the Ryane rear coil-over conversion kit too. Phil, which strut cartridge did you use? The Bilstein sure are nice, but is there a cheaper alternative that works almost as good? Anyone know what spring rate I should try first if I get the rear coil-overs? It's a 85 SE V8 350 Fiero that will have 255-60-15 rear tires.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Phil||02-18-2000||01:24 PM||||I just got some Gabrial(sp) struts and cut off the spring perch. You could even get replacement cartridges and rebuild the ones you have. As far as I remember on the RCC bump steer kit they move the hinge point out about 1 1/2 in. maybe that is what Rayne is talking about with the "sweet spot" thingy, his may be a better and more forgiving system I don't know.||||reg||1
Z||000009||batboy||02-18-2000||02:06 PM||||Just checked the budget, no way on the $450 Ryane bump steer kit, however, I am getting the Ryane rear coil-overs. I've found Monroe Sensa-Trac and Gabriel Gas Ryder strut cartridges locally for about $100 per pair. Both of these brands are a premium road sensing type of strut and both are fairly equal in quality. Maybe not as good as Bilstein or Koni, but MUCH cheaper.||||reg||1
Z||000010||batboy||02-18-2000||03:11 PM||||I found another possibility. The Fiero Store has those KYB performance suspension kits. The #1 kit includes shocks, struts, and rear swaybar. I'm assuming those KYB struts will work with the coil-over kit. So many options! Fortunately, this time of year we usually have plenty of time to plan, change our mind a couple times, and then finally decide these things.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Phil||02-18-2000||07:09 PM||||On spring rate   the guy a Ryane was very helpful if picking our the right one and he said if I didn't like 'em no problem in exchanging them.||||reg||1
Z||000012||pherder||02-18-2000||07:26 PM||||Guys,<P>You have to compair apples to apples ... not apples to ibms!!!<P>The (used to be) $220 KIT from RMS is JUST the spring, top hat, threaded tube and the "nut" that the spring sits on.  You have to "remove" the spring perch off of whatever strut you decide to use.  When you buy the $395 coil overs from RCC you get a Grabial gas strut that has already been modified (spring perch cut off) and the rest of the pieces put together.  It took me lots of time, grief, swearing, etc. to carve off the spring perches WITHOUT cutting into the strut it's self and maybe letting the gas out and ruining the strut.  If your time is worth less than the cost of having either RCC or RMS modify a strut to fit then buy RMS's kit.  <P>I have had or have coil over suspensions from BOTH companies.  BOTH companies sell high quality parts.<P>I sold my 86 Fiero just as RCC came out with their bump steer kit otherwise I would have been their "tester" out at the autocross events.<P>A Fiero racer (George Ryan) who has a Quad 4 race engine in an 84 Fiero, put one of these RCC bump steer kits in his race car and even though he only had 1" of travel, it made a big difference!  <P>One more difference between RCC and RMS, time it takes to get parts ... RCC will be quick, RMS ... well I waited over 6 months to get my 88 coil over suspension with tubular control arms.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>Paul<BR><p>[This message has been edited by pherder (edited 02-18-2000).]||||reg||4
Z||000013||lowCG||02-18-2000||11:14 PM||||Sounds like(serious V8)torque steer to me;bump steer is when the car will move around,requiring steering input to correct,after hitting a bump in the road.It's usually caused by having the steering tie-rod change position(poor rack positioning,or spindle pick-up points).Suspension geometry can affect this also,front and rear.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000014||Monkeyman||02-18-2000||11:28 PM||||Now I'm confused!||||reg||1
Z||000015||batboy||02-19-2000||12:33 AM||||Ok, I have bump steer too, but what I was describing earlier was torque steer. Once, I saw a bum steer take a dump and boy was I torqued.<P>For coil-over spring rates, here is a rule of thumb for Fiero, from what I was told. I personally don't know.<P>250# similar to stock<BR>300# semi-performance<BR>350# high performance<BR>400# competition||||reg||1
Z||000016||theogre||02-19-2000||11:55 AM||||Bats,<P>What you are describing above when you hit the gas is likely not a bump steering problem. This is a common problem in Fiero, especially in manual trany cars. Even the 4 cyls will do it.<P>It's caused by the mounting location of the OEM torque strut(dogbone) and engine torque. When the engine load changes it pushes or pulls on the strut. Problem is the strut is located off center and connects to the car body while the rest of the motor connects to the cradle. When the motor loads up on the strut it twists the cradle because the right side gets more load than the left. <P>So what you get is the right side of the cradle being shoved forwards or backwards twisting the whole rear end of the car like a set of wheels on an old Radio Flyer. With a V8 even Poly cradle bushings may not stop it.||||reg||1
Z||000017||Phil||02-19-2000||12:33 PM||||pherder<BR>Good point on the strut $$. I didn't think of that as I had just replaced my struts before I decided to go with coil-overs.||||reg||1
Z||000018||batboy||02-19-2000||09:20 PM||||I like the idea of a complete bolt on coil over conversion. The Rayne unit will still save $30 to $40 depending on which strut cartridge you use, but apparently requires some modification. I wonder if RCC will cut me a good deal if I order both the rear coil-overs and the bump steer kit together? If I'm leaning hard into a corner and hit a bump, the Batmobile does get a little skittish (skidish?), so I'm assuming I have both bump steer and torque steer problems. I'm hoping that the new rear poly control arm bushings and new rear ball joints will help a little with the torque steer. If I'm still not happy, I guess the next step would be replacing the dogbone and cradle bushings with poly too. What about motor and tranny mounts? Does anyone make poly mounts for the Fiero? If so, would these help some?||||reg||1
Z||000019||Monkeyman||02-19-2000||09:41 PM||||"Skittish" is correct, although it sounds like the other might work just as well in your case.||||reg||1
Z||000020||cobra427||02-19-2000||10:08 PM||||B/B,Is your dogbone located in the same general location as the original? My reason for asking is that I have seen v-8 Fieros with a torque rod placed at the rear of the engine.(transmission area)and attached to the trunk side with a reinforcing plate to help keep the engine from dancing around. If my memory serves me, it was a turnbuckle type thingy mounted solidly at both ends. Between the two torque rods, you might be able to improve on that unwanted torque steer.||||reg||1
Z||000021||pherder||02-19-2000||10:46 PM||||BB,<P>To rid yourself of torque steer, replace the rubber bushings in the cradle with metal ones!  That will keep the cradle from shifting during acceleration.  <P>If you are wondering if there will be a problem solid mounting the cradle, the 88 cradles are solid mounted.<P>You will get more vibration from having it solid mounted (along with having the V8 solid mounted) but the feel of the car without the extra rear end movement will convince you.<P>Get the bump steer kit also.  It is really weird seeing the rear wheels tow in and out as the wheels go up and down.  (They tow out when the strut is compressed and then go back to neutral at normal ride height and then go back to tow out with the wheels hanging down.  <P>So you head into a curve, the body of the car leans over to the outside of the curve, the rear wheel which is at that point, doing all the work in the rear is now towed out and helping the rear end try to catch up to the front end!  Add to that the torque steer from all that V8 power and ... can you say interesting driving???<P>Paul||||reg||4
Z||000022||batboy||02-20-2000||12:04 AM||||Cobra, the V8 dogbone is mounted in the same place as the old V6 one, no rear torque rod. I do remember seeing two dogbones on a 1990 Grand Prix with a 3.1 engine. Hmmmm, I wonder... maybe a second dogbone/torque rod might not be a bad idea. I found this pic of the V8 Archie's torque rod that you were talking about. Any thoughts about using both? Will it help solve the problem or will it create more.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.v8archie.com/67.jpg"> <P>I'm going to try everything else before I consider changing the cradle bushings, which were new (factory rubber style) at the time of the engine swap. Paul mentioned solid engine mounts, I guess I forgot that's how they mount those V8 in there. I believe the tranny mounts are rubber though. If the motor mounts are solid, I'm not sure about solid cradle bushings. The 88 cradles may of been mounted solid, but they still retained rubber motor mounts.<P>Thanks for all the ideas and suggestions guys. I like this "think tank" approach to problem solving. I never claimed to be an expert on V8 Fiero suspensions, but I'm learning.||||reg||1
Z||000023||pherder||02-20-2000||10:35 AM||||BB,<P>The V8 engines using V8Archie's kit are solid mounted in the front no matter the year of the Fiero.<P>So, my 88 Formula will have a solid mount in the front and the cradle will be solid mounted.<P>BTW, I WOULD add the rear torque bar since the front one isn't really doing anything EXCEPT keeping the front mount from breaking off!  The front of the engine is solid mounted.  It is the REAR of the engine, with the stock rubber transaxle mounts that is moving around!<P>Paul||||reg||11
Z||000024||batboy||02-20-2000||01:11 PM||||Yes, it's all becoming very clear now. Thanks Paul! That V8 Archie's torque rod look easy enough to make. Two heim ends with an adjustable center rod like they use on traction bars ought to work just fine. I'll mount it in about the same place as the pic shows, except I have to work around the exhaust pipe where it exits the manifold. The V8 Archie method of installing this torque rod looks like one end bolts to the "back" of the head and the other end to the cradle. I will have to fabricate the cradle mounting bracket. Shouldn't be too tough to make and that should help keep that fire breathing V8 engine in place.<P>Paul, I'm helping some friends that live west of Wichita move next weekend (I think), maybe we can meet for lunch or something if I have some spare time. I sure would like to see your projects. Email me with your phone number if this is a possibility. We gotta get Dogtired in on this too. Sound like a pretty nice day out today (Sunday the 19th). Going outside to do some Fiero tinkering after lunch.||||reg||1
Z||000025||theogre||02-20-2000||01:25 PM||||that was interesting reading...... solid mounting an engine in a street car is generally considered a not goot idea.<P>In a "normal" setup, with properly installed mounts, the motor/tranny set moving around will not cause a handling problem. It's only when the motor/tranny set can move the cradle that you would have the torque steer I describe above. normal movement is isolated from the wheels by the half shafts.<P>Another type of torque effect, when the ball joint end of the A-arm moves due to bushing compression, is usually a balanced effect. It will change the alignment, but ususally it's pretty even so the car still goes where you aim it. This could affect cornering if torque load changes suddenly.<P>Solid or polly cradle mounts with a solid engine mount is likely to shake pretty good. It depends on just what motor is involved and how it's tuned, etc. I think I would have taken a diferant aproach to this. (Just putting polly in the dog bone in my 87 4 cyl automatic increased vibrating considerably. Unfortunatly with the single tranny mount used after 86.5 for the automatic polly dogbone is a requirement unless you like the air cleaner beating the trunk wall.)<P>For example a common thing with inline setups for RWD cars is to put a chain or cable across the left mount to protect in under high load. Good mounts for V8's typically have an internal reinforcement that can eliminate the need for the chain with all but the biggest motors. Some cars even came factory with a cable across the left mount. (Cable won't rattle like a chain can, and it's easier to make cable an exact lenght.)<P>In a setup like Fiero I think I would have put a cable across both front mounts. Maybe even all of them in a stick car. That would let the motor move normally and isolate vibration, but prevent mount breakage under hard accel/deccel loading.<P>The long strut in the picture above isn't a bad idea. Only problem I have with it is this. If it gets bent even a little it could fail very suddenly when it's under compression. (review the properties of a cylinder under axial compresstion/tension.) Such a long strut is pretty easy to get bent. Bent even a little would seriously reduce it's effeciveness even if it doesn't collapse.<P>Someones likely to ask why the've never seen the a factory cable. 1. it's short. It runs across the left mount and no one ever pays attention the mounts unless it's an engine swap. 2. the cable is not needed with the internally reinforced mounts in common use today. Only huge motors can break these mounts.||||reg||1
Z||000026||batboy||02-20-2000||02:34 PM||||Ogre, I used to chain down the motor mounts on my Chevelle SS396 back in my youth. You sure don't want a big block coming loose during a hole shot, <B>whoa</B>! Also, good point about using a sturdy torque rod. I was thinking of ordering a racing traction bar adjustable link and heim joints from JEGS or some place similar. I figure those have to be "aircraft quality" heavy duty stuff if they use them for drag racing.<P>Another question. Mounting the lower end of the torque bar onto the cradle like in that V8 Archie's pic is beginning to sound like it won't effectively cure the problem. It will still cause the cradle to move under hard acceleration. Maybe Cobra's idea of mounting it to a reinforced part of the trunk or even to the space frame would be a better idea. What about using a poly bushing on one end of the torque rod and a heim joint on the other. Questions, questions, questions!||||reg||1
Z||000027||pherder||02-20-2000||03:13 PM||||theorge,<P>One thing you don't address is that the V8 kits use what was the 55 Chevy V8 engine mounts, i.e. the dual holes just under the water pump mounts.  They do not use the "normal" engine mounts since the Fiero's axle passes right thru that area!<P>This flat plate (looks like a "U") is bolted to an 90 degree angle piece that is welded to the cradle.<P>I agree it isn't the best solution but it works ...  I don't like that the front is solid but the back isn't.  I tried to have the front poly mounted and used a Chevy poly replacement transmission mount as the bottom of the "U" mount instead of bolting the "U" to the welded solid mount (i.e. had a mount made just outside of the cradle between the two suspension links and bolted the transmission mount there and then the angle piece was bolted to it and the "U".  Then I had two of those torque struts made to bolt to both ends of the heads and from there to the cradle.  They were poly mounted to the cradle but solid mounted to the engine heads.<P>Never got a chance to see if it worked.  Got laid off a job and proceeded to sell the assembly to a Fiero owner in Houston, TX.<P>Paul||||reg||3
